  "interpretationNotes": [
    "nameReferenceCount excludes IdentifierName/GenericName nodes that ARE a declaration's own name in Roslyn's syntax tree (only NamespaceDeclaration/FileScopedNamespaceDeclaration.Name and a using-alias's own NameEquals.Name are represented this way; verified by hand-trace on a synthetic sample, see QA report). All other declaration names (type/method/property/etc.) are plain identifier tokens, not IdentifierName/GenericName nodes, so no further exclusion was needed or applied.",
    "Generated-path exclusion for NAV candidates and DIFF file-count/line-count checks applies literally the five patterns in protocol.md R2-2 rule 1 (*.g.cs, *.Designer.cs, *.generated.cs, /Generated/, /obj/) via case-insensitive suffix/substring match on the corpus-relative path. Files with other 'generated-looking' names that do not match these five literal patterns (e.g. '*.g.verified.cs' snapshot test fixtures found in large-aspnetcore) are NOT excluded, since the protocol text specifies exact patterns rather than a general heuristic.",
    "select-diff's DiffCsNumstat step uses `git diff --numstat` with git's own default rename-detection behavior (no -M, no --no-renames), per team-lead direction: R2-2 rules 2/3 say literally 'git diff --numstat' with no flag, and on this tool's git installation (2.50.1.windows.1, diff.renames unset everywhere) that default is empirically rename detection ON (confirmed identical to an explicit -M on a synthetic rename). Rename records are parsed unambiguously via `-z` (NUL-delimited output) rather than the human-readable 'old => new' / 'dir/{old => new}/rest' compaction, and are accounted under the new (post-rename) path as a single changed file. An earlier implementation forced `--no-renames`; recomputing all three corpora with the default-rename-detection implementation produced byte-identical final 6 pairs and rejected-candidate lists for every corpus (see each corpus's `diffPairs.renameDetectionCheck`) — only `eligibleCommitCount` shifted by 1-2 (commits that were not needed to fill the 6 pairs either way).",
    "For medium-quartznet and large-aspnetcore, `core.autocrlf=false` alone left CRLF line endings in the working tree (confirmed by byte-level diff against `git show <commit>:<path>`), because both repositories' .gitattributes set `* text=auto`, which makes checkout line-ending normalization follow `core.eol` (default native/CRLF on Windows) regardless of core.autocrlf. Fixed by also setting `core.eol=lf` and forcing a full re-checkout; verified byte-exact afterwards (see each corpus's `checkout` block). This is a general risk for any future corpus acquisition (e.g. TASK-029) on Windows and is documented in `benchmarks/corpora/tools/README.md`.",
    "The DIFF commit-pair task cards' provenance note (pair k's p sometimes equals pair k+1's c') reflects the sliding-window algorithm continuing from the next unused eligible commit after each accepted pair; it is expected, not a defect.",
    "Each corpus's `historyObservations` records only directly observed facts about its pinned first-parent-500 commit range, root-level file listing, and committer timestamps (per team-lead direction) — no characterization of why those facts exist. All three corpora are the actual GitHub upstream repositories pinned by TASK-023/protocol.md R2-1; this task re-verified the pinned commit SHA (`git rev-parse HEAD` after checkout) and the pinned-commit LICENSE content locally, but did not re-query the GitHub API (that was TASK-023/024's step, see corpus-candidates.md)."
  ]
